        {
          "subsection_id": "laryngoscope_subsection_2",
          "subsection_title": "1. Introduction",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "laryngoscope_group_2",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A laryngoscope is an instrument used to visualise the larynx and vocal cords, most commonly to facilitate endotracheal intubation during general anaesthesia, resuscitation and emergency airway management."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "It is used in anaesthesia, emergency medicine, intensive care and airway management."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Key OSCE identification point: A laryngoscope typically consists of a handle and a detachable blade with a light source. Modern video laryngoscopes incorporate a camera and display to assist visualisation."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The laryngoscope is primarily a visualisation instrument; the endotracheal tube is the device passed through the vocal cords to secure the airway."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "laryngoscope_subsection_3",
          "subsection_title": "2. Classification",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "laryngoscope_group_3",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Laryngoscopes can be classified according to blade design and viewing method."
                },
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Direct laryngoscope: The operator looks directly through the mouth to visualise the larynx.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Video laryngoscope: A camera at or near the blade tip displays the airway on a screen.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Flexible endoscopic scope: A flexible optical instrument that can facilitate selected airway procedures, including difficult airway management.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "For basic surgical instrument OSCEs, the most important distinction is between Macintosh curved blades and Miller straight blades."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "laryngoscope_subsection_4",
          "subsection_title": "3. Parts and labelling",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "laryngoscope_group_4",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/laryngoscope_03.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "flipkart.com"
                },
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "Part",
                    "Function"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "Handle",
                      "Provides grip and houses the power source in many conventional models."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Blade",
                      "Retracts the tongue and helps expose the larynx."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Blade tip",
                      "Distal end used to position the blade during laryngoscopy."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Flange",
                      "Blade projection that helps displace soft tissue and the tongue."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Light source",
                      "Illuminates the airway."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Hinge or attachment point",
                      "Connects the detachable blade to the handle."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Battery or power supply",
                      "Provides power to the illumination system."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Light contacts",
                      "Transfer electrical power between the handle and blade in compatible designs."
                    ]
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Exam tip: Some modern laryngoscopes use fibre-optic illumination or integrated LEDs rather than a conventional bulb. Video laryngoscopes also include a camera, display and associated electronic components."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "laryngoscope_subsection_5",
          "subsection_title": "4. Types of laryngoscope blades",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "laryngoscope_group_5",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/laryngoscope_04.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A. Macintosh blade — curved"
                },
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "The most commonly used conventional direct laryngoscope blade in adults.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "The tip is positioned in the vallecula, the space between the base of the tongue and the epiglottis.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Lifting the blade indirectly elevates the epiglottis through tension on the hyoepiglottic ligament.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Available in different sizes.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/laryngoscope_05.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "B. Miller blade — straight"
                },
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Commonly used in neonatal and paediatric airway management, although it can also be used in adults.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "The tip is used to lift the epiglottis directly.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "May be useful when the epiglottis is relatively large or floppy.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Requires appropriate technique and blade selection.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/laryngoscope_06.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "C. McCoy blade — hinged tip"
                },
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Has a hinged distal tip controlled by a lever.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Allows additional elevation of the epiglottis when required.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "May be useful in selected difficult laryngoscopy situations.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Does not guarantee successful intubation.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/laryngoscope_07.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "D. Video laryngoscope blade"
                },
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Incorporates a camera near the distal blade.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Displays the laryngeal view on a screen.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "May improve glottic visualisation in many difficult airway situations.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Requires familiarity with the specific device and its tube-delivery technique.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "laryngoscope_subsection_6",
          "subsection_title": "5. Macintosh versus Miller: high-yield comparison",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "laryngoscope_group_6",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "Feature",
                    "Macintosh",
                    "Miller"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "Blade shape",
                      "Curved",
                      "Straight"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Tip position",
                      "Vallecula",
                      "Usually lifts epiglottis directly"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Epiglottis elevation",
                      "Indirect",
                      "Direct"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Common application",
                      "Adult direct laryngoscopy",
                      "Often neonatal and paediatric laryngoscopy"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Main principle",
                      "Tension through the hyoepiglottic ligament",
                      "Direct elevation of the epiglottis"
                    ]
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Mnemonic: Macintosh — vallecula; Miller — lifts the epiglottis."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "laryngoscope_subsection_7",
          "subsection_title": "6. Principle of operation",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "laryngoscope_group_7",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The laryngoscope provides a line of sight to the glottis by displacing the tongue and associated soft tissues."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "With a Macintosh blade, the tip is placed in the vallecula and the blade is lifted to elevate the epiglottis indirectly. With a Miller blade, the tip is used to lift the epiglottis directly."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The blade should be lifted in the direction of its handle to elevate the tissues, not levered against the upper teeth."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The aim is to visualise the vocal cords sufficiently to allow safe passage of an endotracheal tube when intubation is indicated."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "laryngoscope_subsection_8",
          "subsection_title": "7. Indications and clinical uses",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "laryngoscope_group_8",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Endotracheal intubation during general anaesthesia.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Emergency airway management during resuscitation.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Airway control in selected critically ill patients.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Assessment of laryngeal anatomy during direct laryngoscopy.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Selected airway procedures under specialist supervision.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Assistance with removal of certain visible upper-airway foreign bodies by trained clinicians using appropriate equipment.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A laryngoscope is not required for every airway intervention. Bag-mask ventilation and supraglottic airway devices may be appropriate alternatives or rescue options depending on the clinical situation."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "laryngoscope_subsection_9",
          "subsection_title": "8. Advantages",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "laryngoscope_group_9",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Provides direct visualisation of the larynx and vocal cords.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Facilitates endotracheal intubation.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Conventional models are portable and reusable.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Different blade sizes accommodate different patient anatomies.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Video laryngoscopy can improve the view of the glottis in many patients.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Allows airway assessment during selected emergency and anaesthetic procedures.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "laryngoscope_subsection_10",
          "subsection_title": "9. Disadvantages",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "laryngoscope_group_10",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Direct laryngoscopy requires appropriate positioning, technique and operator experience.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "A poor laryngeal view may make intubation difficult.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Excessive force can injure the teeth, lips, tongue or pharynx.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Direct laryngoscopy may provoke sympathetic stimulation, causing tachycardia and hypertension.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Video laryngoscopes are more expensive and depend on functioning electronics and power.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "A good video view does not necessarily mean the endotracheal tube can be passed easily.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Blood, vomit and secretions can obscure the view, particularly with video devices.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "laryngoscope_subsection_11",
          "subsection_title": "10. Contraindications and precautions",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "laryngoscope_group_11",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "There is no single absolute contraindication to laryngoscopy when a life-saving airway intervention is required. However, precautions include:"
                },
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Suspected cervical spine injury: minimise neck movement and use an appropriate airway strategy.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Restricted mouth opening or severe facial trauma: select an alternative airway approach when indicated.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Significant upper-airway obstruction: anticipate deterioration and obtain expert airway assistance.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Loose teeth, dental disease or dental prostheses: assess the risk of dental injury.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Full stomach or high aspiration risk: follow the appropriate airway and aspiration-prevention strategy.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Known difficult airway: prepare an appropriate primary and rescue plan before attempting intubation.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "In a difficult airway, repeated unsuccessful attempts can cause bleeding, oedema and loss of the ability to oxygenate. Prioritise oxygenation and follow the relevant airway management algorithm."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "laryngoscope_subsection_12",
          "subsection_title": "11. Pre-use checks",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "laryngoscope_group_12",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "title": "Before using a conventional laryngoscope, check:",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Blade integrity: No cracks, deformation, sharp edges or loose components.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "1."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Attachment: The blade locks securely onto the handle.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "2."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Illumination: The light works and is sufficiently bright.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "3."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Power supply: Batteries or the rechargeable power source are functional.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "4."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Cleanliness: The device has been processed according to the manufacturer's instructions.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "5."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Correct size: Select a blade appropriate for the patient's anatomy.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "6."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Backup equipment: Ensure an alternative blade, video laryngoscope where available, suction and rescue airway equipment are accessible.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "7."
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "For a video laryngoscope, additionally check the screen, camera, image quality, battery and compatibility of the blade and tube-delivery equipment."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "laryngoscope_subsection_13",
          "subsection_title": "12. Direct laryngoscopy: OSCE demonstration",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "laryngoscope_group_13",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/laryngoscope_08.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Step 1 — Preparation"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Confirm the indication, assess the airway, explain the procedure when possible, obtain consent when feasible, and prepare oxygen, suction, monitoring, endotracheal tubes and rescue airway equipment. Ensure an appropriately trained assistant is available."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/laryngoscope_09.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Step 2 — Positioning and preoxygenation"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Preoxygenate the patient and position appropriately. A sniffing position may be suitable for many adults without cervical spine concerns; a ramped position may be beneficial in some patients. If cervical spine injury is suspected, minimise neck movement and use the appropriate precautions."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/laryngoscope_10.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Step 3 — Blade insertion"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A trained operator introduces the blade into the mouth under direct vision, displacing the tongue appropriately. With a Macintosh blade, the tip is advanced into the vallecula; with a Miller blade, the tip is used to lift the epiglottis directly."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/laryngoscope_11.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Step 4 — Visualisation"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Lift the blade in the appropriate direction without levering against the teeth. Identify the epiglottis and glottic opening. If the view is inadequate, use appropriate corrective manoeuvres or an alternative technique rather than applying excessive force."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/laryngoscope_12.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Step 5 — Intubation and confirmation"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "If indicated, the trained operator passes the endotracheal tube through the vocal cords, removes the laryngoscope and confirms placement using continuous waveform capnography where available, alongside clinical assessment. Secure the tube and continue monitoring."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Critical safety point: Endotracheal intubation is a high-risk procedure requiring appropriate training and supervision. Tube placement must be confirmed; visualisation during insertion alone is not sufficient."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "laryngoscope_subsection_14",
          "subsection_title": "13. Complications",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "laryngoscope_group_14",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "Complication",
                    "Mechanism or cause"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "Dental injury",
                      "Excessive force or levering against the teeth."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Lip or tongue trauma",
                      "Tissue becomes trapped between the blade and teeth."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Pharyngeal or laryngeal injury",
                      "Difficult or forceful instrumentation."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Hypoxaemia",
                      "Prolonged attempts or inadequate ventilation."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Aspiration",
                      "Regurgitation of gastric contents into the airway."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Tachycardia and hypertension",
                      "Sympathetic response to laryngoscopy."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Bradycardia or arrhythmias",
                      "Hypoxia, vagal stimulation or other physiological disturbances."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Failed intubation",
                      "Inadequate view, difficult anatomy or unsuccessful tube delivery."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Cervical spine movement",
                      "Potential concern in patients with suspected cervical spine injury."
                    ]
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "laryngoscope_subsection_15",
          "subsection_title": "14. High-yield OSCE viva questions",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "laryngoscope_group_15",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q1. Identify this instrument."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A laryngoscope, used to visualise the larynx and facilitate endotracheal intubation."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q2. Name the two common direct laryngoscope blades."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Macintosh, which is curved, and Miller, which is straight."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q3. Where is the Macintosh blade tip placed?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "In the vallecula, to elevate the epiglottis indirectly."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q4. How does the Miller blade elevate the epiglottis?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "It generally lifts the epiglottis directly."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q5. Why should the blade not be levered against the upper teeth?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "This can cause dental injury and does not represent the correct lifting technique."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q6. What is the difference between direct and video laryngoscopy?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Direct laryngoscopy relies on the operator's direct line of sight; video laryngoscopy uses a camera and screen to display the airway."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q7. How do you confirm endotracheal tube placement?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Continuous waveform capnography is the principal method for confirming tracheal placement in standard circumstances, supplemented by clinical assessment."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q8. What should you do if intubation fails?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Call for assistance, prioritise oxygenation, limit repeated traumatic attempts and follow the appropriate difficult-airway algorithm, including rescue ventilation and alternative airway devices."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q9. What are the functions of the handle?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "It provides grip and, in many conventional models, houses the battery or power supply for illumination."
                }
